---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MetricCard from "../components/MetricCard.astro";
import FigurePanel from "../components/FigurePanel.astro";
import data from "../data/results.json";

const passCount = `${data.headline.splitHypothesesPass} / ${data.headline.splitHypothesesTotal}`;
const h5Ci = `[${data.h5Live.ci[0]}, ${data.h5Live.ci[1]}]`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pratyabhijna World Model"
  description="Evidence-first companion site for the Pratyabhijna World Model: static text world models, active inference, and an honest H5b negative result."
>
  <section class="hero" id="overview">
    <div class="heroCopy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pratyabhijna World Model</p>
      <h1>A world-model substrate for creative AI, with the negative result kept in view.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        PWM operationalises Kashmir Saiva recognition philosophy as a Dreamer-class textual world model:
        latent imagination, Expected Free Energy, Hopfield memory, sleep consolidation, and a gated LLM bridge.
        9 of 10 split hypotheses pass; the live text-only comparison against a 120B LLM failed.
      </p>
      <div class="heroActions">
        <a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/SharathSPhD/pratyabhijna-world-model">GitHub repository</a>
        <a class="button" href="#results">Read the results</a>
      </div>
      <p class="sourceNote">
        Page built from checked-in JSON under <code>benchmarks/results/</code>; generated {new Date(data.generatedAt).toUTCString()}.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="heroPanel" aria-label="Headline status">
      <MetricCard
        kicker="Split hypothesis ledger"
        title="Internal gates mostly pass"
        value={passCount}
        note="H5 is split into H5a internal-imagination PASS and H5b live-text FAIL."
        tone="caveat"
      />
      <MetricCard
        kicker="H1"
        title="EFE over REINFORCE"
        value={`${data.headline.h1RewardRatio}x`}
        note="Mean episode reward ratio in the Phase 2 gate."
        tone="pass"
      />
      <MetricCard
        kicker="H5b"
        title="Bare 120B wins live text"
        value={`g=${data.headline.h5bHedgesG}`}
        note="PWM conditioning did not improve text-only camatkara score."
        tone="fail"
      />
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section proseBlock">
    <h2>Plain-language reading</h2>
    <p>
      PWM is not another prompt wrapper. It tries to give creative generation a latent world model:
      an internal state that can be observed, imagined forward, rewarded, remembered, consolidated, and
      only then narrated through a frozen LLM. That matters because LLM-only creative systems often hide
      the mechanism of revision inside opaque text generation.
    </p>
    <p>
      The project did not prove that world-model conditioning beats a frontier-scale language model at final
      text quality. It showed something narrower and more useful: static creative text can be turned into a
      hostile but workable world-model training substrate, and the resulting internal process can be measured
      separately from the final surface text.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="results">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Evidence ledger</p>
      <h2>Results at a glance</h2>
      <p>
        These cards separate pass, failure, and caveat states. Each metric names the source JSON that produced it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="resultGrid">
      {data.hypotheses.map((h) => (
        <article class={`resultCard ${h.status}`}>
          <div class="resultTop">
            <span class="resultId">{h.id}</span>
            <span class={`status ${h.status}`}>{h.status}</span>
          </div>
          <h3>{h.label}</h3>
          <p class="metric">{h.metric}</p>
          <p>{h.caveat}</p>
          <small>{h.source}</small>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split" id="h5b">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">H5b live ablation</p>
      <h2>H5b is a real negative result, not a footnote.</h2>
      <p>
        The live protocol compared PWM-conditioned generation against a bare <code>nemotron-3-super:120b</code>
        baseline with a text-only camatkara scorer. The bare LLM scored higher:
        PWM {data.h5Live.meanPwm}, LLM {data.h5Live.meanLlm}, Hedges' g {data.h5Live.hedgesG},
        BCa 95% CI {h5Ci}, permutation p {data.h5Live.pPermutation}.
      </p>
      <p>
        The right interpretation is boundary, not dismissal. The scorer deliberately excludes VFE and other
        world-model-only terms; the 120B baseline is near ceiling in English-script domains; Kannada film lyrics
        show near parity. Internal process gains are real, but they do not yet guarantee better surface text.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="tableCard">
      <h3>Per-domain means</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Domain</th><th>PWM</th><th>LLM</th><th>Wins</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.h5Live.domainRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.domain.replaceAll("_", " ")}</td>
              <td>{row.pwmMean}</td>
              <td>{row.llmMean}</td>
              <td>{row.pwmWins} / {row.llmWins}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="sourceNote">Source: <code>benchmarks/results/h5_live_ablation.json</code></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="architecture">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
      <h2>The philosophical conjecture as a call stack</h2>
      <p>
        The Sanskrit terms are not used as decorative labels here; they shape which state is shared,
        when the LLM is invoked, and what counts as a creative event.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div><strong>Static corpora</strong><span>public text, no natural action loop</span></div>
      <div><strong>Trika RSSM</strong><span>latent h/z state over text observations</span></div>
      <div><strong>EFE + camatkara</strong><span>policy and intrinsic reward</span></div>
      <div><strong>Citta + sleep</strong><span>Hopfield retrieval and consolidation</span></div>
      <div><strong>Vimarsha bridge</strong><span>world-model state biases the LLM</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Adversarial reading</h3>
      <p>
        The design grammar is coherent, but the evidence does not yet prove that Kashmir Saiva categories
        are uniquely necessary or that humans prefer PWM outputs. The strongest claim is that the philosophy
        generated a productive, testable architecture.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section visuals" id="showcase">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Paper visuals</p>
      <h2>Figures carried into the public site</h2>
      <p>
        The site reuses paper figures and new journal-revision figures so the non-technical page remains tied
        to the same data artifacts as the manuscript.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="figureGrid">
      <FigurePanel src="fig_system_overview.png" title="System overview" caption="Pancakrtya loop, world model, reward, memory, sleep, and bridge." />
      <FigurePanel src="fig_h1_efe_vs_reinforce.png" title="H1 EFE vs REINFORCE" caption="The strongest internal active-inference result." />
      <FigurePanel src="fig10_h5_live_per_domain.png" title="H5b live per-domain" caption="The negative live text-only ablation." />
      <FigurePanel src="fig11_a6_ablation.png" title="A6 hierarchy ablation" caption="Three-level Trika against one-level world model." />
      <FigurePanel src="fig12_ttft_warm_cold.png" title="TTFT warm/cold" caption="Warm-path evidence and cold-start confounds." />
      <FigurePanel src="fig_failure_chain.png" title="H1 failure chain" caption="The passive-corpus diagnostic cascade." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Breakthroughs under review</p>
      <h2>What deserves the strongest future vetting?</h2>
      <ul class="checkList">
        <li><strong>29.72x H1</strong>: strong internal result, but tied to project reward protocol.</li>
        <li><strong>{data.a6.advantageFactor}x H7/A6</strong>: promising hierarchy signal, but two seeds are partial.</li>
        <li><strong>Vimarsha bridge</strong>: learns a mapping, but H5b says the mapping does not yet beat bare 120B text output.</li>
        <li><strong>TTFT cascade</strong>: product mechanism works in tests; aggregate live validation still fails.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="tableCard">
      <h3>A6 seeds</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Seed</th><th>Status</th><th>Steps</th><th>VFE</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.a6.seeds.map((seed) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{seed.seed}</td>
              <td>{seed.status}</td>
              <td>{seed.steps}</td>
              <td>{seed.finalVfe}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="reproducibility">
    <div class="sectionHeader">
      <p class="eyebrow">Reproducibility</p>
      <h2>Every public number should point to a file.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="reproGrid">
      {data.sourceFiles.map((source) => (
        <code>{source}</code>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Known limits</h3>
      <p>
        Full 120B local inference requires local model weights and checkpoint paths that are not shipped in this repository.
        The most reproducible public layer is the source code, paper, figure scripts, and JSON result artifacts.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
